## 2. EXISTING WEBSITE INSPECTION AND MIGRATION

Before building, inspect the current Nyota One website and any source code, repository or assets supplied with this project.

Identify:
- Existing pages, URLs and navigation.
- Every existing blog post and article.
- Existing projects and live application links.
- Current services, pricing and product listings.
- Existing images, logos, brand assets and downloadable resources.
- Existing forms, contact links, WhatsApp links and integrations.
- Existing SEO metadata, indexed URLs and sitemap.
- Any working customer accounts, admin features or payment integrations.

Use the actual existing website and source material as the migration source. Do not assume that a list of six featured projects represents the entire portfolio.

### Migration rules

1. Preserve every recoverable existing blog post, including its title, body, slug, publication date, author, categories, tags, featured image, SEO title, meta description and internal links where available.
2. Preserve all existing portfolio projects and their working URLs.
3. Preserve existing service pages, pricing information and useful content unless a specific improvement is required.
4. Preserve important URLs. If a URL must change, create a permanent 301 redirect from the old URL to the appropriate new URL.
5. Import existing content into the new database or content system rather than hardcoding it into page components.
6. Do not delete, overwrite or silently replace existing content during migration.
7. Where content cannot be retrieved, create a migration report identifying the missing content. Do not invent replacement articles and claim they were migrated.
8. If the current website is inaccessible, request an export, source repository or content files and continue building the other modules with clearly identified sample data.
9. Keep a migration log with source URL, destination URL, migration status and redirect status.
10. Provide a final content reconciliation report listing migrated, updated, redirected, missing and unpublished items.

The current production website must remain available while the replacement is being built and tested.

